WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:04.650
Watch as I knit my RTR hat.

2
00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:09.700
Now, I know it's a funny name, but you'll
quickly see why it's named RTR.

3
00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:14.140
Use this watch cap as
inspiration for your next hat.

4
00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:17.180
It has a reverse stockinette Hung hem,

5
00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:24.060
a couple of reverse stockinette amd 1x1
rib inserts it's a 4 gore cap and it's

6
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.220
gathered at the top,
allowing for a ponytail.

7
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:34.160
When you build your pattern,
be sure to account for negative ease.

8
00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:43.140
My mannequin's head is 22 inches,

9
00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:49.380
so I built my pattern to a finished size
of 20 inches. Before I started knitting,

10
00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:54.060
I also sketched out my texture
stripes. The body of my hat,

11
00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:58.140
the H dimension in my pattern was 43 rows,

12
00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:03.540
so I decided that each of my stripes would
be six rows alternating stockinette,

13
00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:08.620
reverse stockinette and hand
manipulated 1x1 ribbing.

14
00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:12.770
Start by casting on with waste
yarn and ravel cord.

15
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:15.540
I knit for about an inch and a half.

16
00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:18.020
Then I turned the tension to the loosest

17
00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:23.460
setting (the largest number) and I also
released the yarn from the tension wire

18
00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:27.660
and held the working yarn
by hand and knit one row.

19
00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:32.760
This created a really loose turning row.

20
00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:39.420
For the second half I knit the same
number of rows for the hem.

21
00:01:39.450 --> 00:01:43.540
For more tips about hems,
especially one of my favorites of picot

22
00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:50.000
hem, please visit the Knit It Now
classroom "Hem and Edges".

23
00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:58.840
To create a hem with the purl side
showing, remove, turn and rehang the hem.

24
00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.620
Now, I don't have a garter bar for this

25
00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:08.920
machine, so I used a circular hand
knitting needle to remove the stitches.

26
00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:13.580
I turned the work so that the knit side

27
00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:20.200
was facing me and then rehung each
of the stitches back in the needles.

28
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.610
Once the stitches were hung back

29
00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:36.600
on the machine, I picked up the open
cast on stitches to hang the hem.

30
00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.900
I then immediately knit my first stripe,

31
00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:48.660
which is stockinette
in contrast to the reverse stockinette hem.

32
00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:50.680


33
00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:57.820
I continued to follow my plan,
and I alternated RTR and hand-reformed one

34
00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:03.360
by one sections to finish
this "H" part of my hat.

35
00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:13.740
At this gauge, re-forming these stitches

36
00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:18.440
wasn't too tedious,
and I really like the texture.

37
00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:47.280
So planning the public side of my
knitting, I started to shape the wedges.

38
00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:49.860
After dividing the number of stitches

39
00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:54.200
according to my pattern,
I scrapped off each section.

40
00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:00.120
To scrap off with my working yarn
at the right, I pulled all the needles

41
00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:05.740
to hold and set my carriage
to holding the working yarn.

42
00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:10.460
I was then able to free pass over
to the left side of my knitting.

43
00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:16.260
I put the first group of needles into work
and then just scrapped off those needles.

44
00:04:16.280 --> 00:04:18.420
I put the second group of needles in work

45
00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:24.200
and scrapped them off and repeated
for the third group of needles.

46
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.060
Then free pass to the right to get
the carriage back to the side

47
00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:39.200
with the working yarn so I can
continue knitting that first wedge.

48
00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:44.320
Now I'm ready to start shaping
the wedge according to my pattern.

49
00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:50.560
I'm decreasing one stitch
on each side every row.

50
00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:53.020
Notice that I'm decreasing two stitches

51
00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.960
in from the edge, and I'm
working a two-step decrease.

52
00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.880
For more shaping tips, visit Knit it Now shaping tutorials.

53
00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:15.660
I want to leave an opening for a ponytail.

54
00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:21.540
So instead of decreasing down to one or
two stitches, as the pattern tells me,

55
00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:28.380
I only decreased down to eight stitches
on each wedge a little over one inch.

56
00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:31.420
I then created eyelets in each wedge

57
00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:37.300
to create holes so I could cinch
up the top of the hat with I-cord.

58
00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:39.820
If you haven't yet knit I-cord,

59
00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:43.420
check the Knit It Now Classroom I-cord
on the machine.

60
00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:45.520
It's easy.

61
00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:47.460
After transferring stitches

62
00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:50.700
for the eyelets,
I continued to knit for about an inch,

63
00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:56.380
and then I bound off each of the wedges
using the I-cord.

64
00:05:56.410 --> 00:05:58.780
The hat can be gathered closed,

65
00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:03.200
or the opening can accommodate
a ponytail or a messy bun.

